(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-nervous-system-motor-quiz"]={"title":"Nervous System: Motor — Physiology Exam 1","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"What is the key functional difference between alpha and gamma motor neurons?","choices":["Gamma motor neurons (large type A-gamma fibers) innervate large extrafusal fibers to generate contractile force; alpha motor neurons (small type A-alpha fibers) innervate small intrafusal fibers to set muscle tone","Alpha motor neurons are smaller in diameter than gamma motor neurons, conducting signals more slowly and innervating only the small intrafusal spindle fibers","Alpha motor neurons innervate large extrafusal fibers (skeletal muscle force); gamma motor neurons innervate small intrafusal fibers in the muscle spindle (controlling muscle tone)","Both alpha and gamma motor neurons innervate the exact same fiber types with identical thresholds, differing only in total synapse count per fiber"],"answer":2,"exp":"Alpha motor neurons (large type A-alpha fibers, ~14 microns) innervate large extrafusal fibers that generate force for muscle contraction, while gamma motor neurons (smaller type A-gamma fibers, ~5 microns) innervate small intrafusal fibers within the muscle spindle to control basic muscle tone. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is a motor unit, as defined via the alpha motor neuron?","choices":["An alpha motor neuron plus all the skeletal muscle fibers it innervates (which can range from 3 to over 100 fibers)","A single gamma motor neuron together with the small intrafusal muscle spindle fibers it exclusively innervates and controls","A single denervated extrafusal muscle fiber with no innervation, generating spontaneous fibrillation potentials due to membrane instability","The muscle spindle alone, considered independently of any surrounding extrafusal fibers or their alpha motor neuron innervation"],"answer":0,"exp":"A motor unit is the alpha motor neuron plus the skeletal (extrafusal) muscle fibers it innervates after branching within the muscle — stimulation can excite anywhere from 3 to over 100 fibers depending on the unit. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the function of Renshaw cells, and what negative feedback mechanism do they mediate?","choices":["They are excitatory glutamatergic interneurons in the anterior horn that amplify alpha motor neuron output, forming a positive feedback loop with no inhibitory component at all","They only affect gamma motor neurons, receiving an excitatory collateral exclusively from gamma axons and feeding back solely to the muscle spindle system","They are inhibitory (glycine-releasing) interneurons that receive an excitatory collateral from an alpha motor neuron and send an inhibitory axon back to that neuron (or others in the same motor pool), mediating negative feedback","They release glutamate to excite motor neurons further, receiving an inhibitory collateral from the alpha motor neuron and amplifying its own output"],"answer":2,"exp":"Renshaw cells are inhibitory interneurons in the anterior horn that release glycine; they receive an excitatory collateral from an alpha motor neuron's axon and send an inhibitory axon back to that neuron (or others in the motor pool), mediating a negative feedback mechanism. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What do the muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ each sense?","choices":["Both structures sense identical information about tendon tension exclusively, differing only in their exact anatomical location within the muscle-tendon unit","The muscle spindle senses muscle length and rate of change in length; the Golgi tendon organ senses tendon tension and rate of change in tension","Neither structure provides feedback to the spinal cord, projecting instead directly and exclusively to the cerebellum for subconscious processing","The muscle spindle senses tendon tension and its rate of change, while the Golgi tendon organ senses muscle length and its rate of change"],"answer":1,"exp":"The muscle spindle (located in the muscle belly) senses muscle length and its rate of change, while the Golgi tendon organ (located in the tendon) senses tendon tension and its rate of change — both feed information mainly to the spinal cord, but also to the cerebrum and cerebellum. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What distinguishes intrafusal muscle fibers from extrafusal fibers?","choices":["Extrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle (innervated by alpha motoneurons) and generate contractile force; intrafusal fibers are smaller, innervated by gamma motoneurons, and encapsulated to form the muscle spindle","Intrafusal fibers are considerably longer than extrafusal fibers, running the entire length of the muscle belly from one tendon to the other","Intrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle, are innervated by alpha motoneurons, and generate the majority of contractile force; extrafusal fibers are smaller, gamma-innervated, and form the encapsulated muscle spindle","Both fiber types are functionally identical, generating equal amounts of contractile force and providing equal sensory feedback to the spinal cord"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extrafusal fibers make up the bulk of the muscle, are innervated by alpha motoneurons, and provide contractile force; intrafusal fibers are smaller, innervated by gamma motoneurons, and are encapsulated in sheaths to form the muscle spindle, running in parallel with (but much shorter than) the extrafusal fibers. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the functional difference between nuclear bag and nuclear chain intrafusal fibers?","choices":["Neither fiber type is innervated by sensory afferents at all; both receive purely motor innervation from gamma motoneurons and play no sensory role","Nuclear chain fibers (Type Ia afferents) detect dynamic changes only, while nuclear bag fibers (Type II afferents) detect static length changes","Nuclear bag fibers (Type Ia afferents) detect rate of change in length (dynamic); nuclear chain fibers (Type II afferents) detect static changes in length","Both fiber types detect only static length changes, with neither one contributing to the dynamic, rate-sensitive response of the spindle"],"answer":2,"exp":"Nuclear bag intrafusal fibers, innervated by Type Ia sensory afferents, detect the rate of change in length (fast, dynamic changes), while nuclear chain intrafusal fibers, innervated by Type II sensory afferents, detect static changes in length. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"How does the muscle spindle oppose an increase in muscle length (stretch)?","choices":["The spindle only responds to muscle shortening rather than stretching, since stretch activates Golgi tendon organs along the tendon exclusively","The spindle's intrafusal fibers directly contract via their own myogenic pacemaker activity without requiring any neural signaling from gamma motoneurons","Stretching the spindle has no effect on alpha motoneurons whatsoever, only altering the firing rate of gamma motoneurons in a separate feedback loop","Stretching the spindle stimulates Type Ia and Type II afferents, which stimulate alpha motoneurons to contract and shorten the entire muscle, opposing the original stretch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stretching a muscle also stretches its spindle, stimulating Type Ia and Type II sensory afferents; Type Ia fibers stimulate alpha-motoneurons in the spinal cord, causing contraction and shortening of the entire muscle, opposing the original stretch. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Why are alpha and gamma motor neurons co-activated during voluntary muscle contraction?","choices":["Co-activation serves no functional purpose at all, since the two motor neuron types fire on entirely independent circuits that never actually interact","To ensure the spindle contracts along with the muscle, keeping the stretch response intact rather than going slack","Only gamma motor neurons are active during voluntary movement, with alpha motor neurons contributing solely during involuntary reflexive contractions","Co-activation prevents any muscle contraction from occurring, since simultaneous alpha and gamma firing produces mutually cancelling inhibitory signals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Alpha and gamma motor neurons are co-activated so that when the main (extrafusal) muscle contracts and shortens, the intrafusal fibers of the spindle also contract, keeping the spindle taut and its stretch response intact — otherwise the spindle would go slack during muscle shortening. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is the difference between the static and dynamic response of the muscle spindle?","choices":["Static and dynamic responses are essentially identical, with both Type Ia and Type II afferents responding equally regardless of how fast the stretch occurs","Only Type II afferents contribute to the dynamic response, firing in direct proportion to the rate of length change during a rapid muscle stretch","The dynamic response only occurs when the muscle is completely stationary, since any ongoing movement suppresses Type Ia afferent firing entirely","Static response: Type Ia and Type II afferent firing increases in proportion to degree of stretch when stretched slowly; dynamic response: Type Ia afferent firing increases in proportion to rate of length change when stretched rapidly"],"answer":3,"exp":"The static response occurs when the spindle is stretched slowly, with Type Ia and Type II afferent impulses increasing proportional to the degree of stretch; the dynamic response occurs with rapid stretch, with Type Ia afferent impulses increasing proportional to the rate of length change. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What type of reflex arc is the stretch (patellar-type) reflex, and what afferent fiber mediates it?","choices":["A disynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type II afferent fibers","A polysynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type Ib afferent fibers","A monosynaptic reflex arc mediated by Type Ia afferent fibers","A reflex arc requiring no afferent fibers at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stretch reflex is a monosynaptic reflex arc: sudden muscle stretch excites the muscle spindle, and Type Ia afferent fibers directly stimulate alpha-motor neurons, causing muscle contraction while synergistic muscles are activated and antagonists inhibited. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In the patellar (knee-jerk) reflex, what happens to the antagonistic hamstring muscle?","choices":["It contracts simultaneously with the quadriceps via shared input","It contracts before the quadriceps via a separate reflex arc","It has no involvement, remaining passive throughout the reflex","It relaxes via an inhibitory interneuron, while the quadriceps contracts"],"answer":3,"exp":"In the patellar reflex, the antagonistic hamstring (flexor) relaxes via an inhibitory interneuron while the Type Ia afferent directly (monosynaptically) stimulates the alpha-motoneuron causing the quadriceps to contract, producing the leg kick. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What type of reflex arc mediates the Golgi tendon organ's autogenic inhibition reflex, and what afferent fiber is involved?","choices":["A disynaptic reflex arc via Type Ib afferent fibers, producing muscle relaxation in response to high tension","A reflex arc using only gamma motor neuron output, adjusting intrafusal fiber tension rather than producing any change in extrafusal muscle force","A monosynaptic reflex arc via Type Ia afferent fibers, producing sudden muscle contraction rather than the protective relaxation actually seen","No reflex arc is involved at all -- it is a purely local mechanical effect confined entirely to the collagen fibers within the tendon itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Golgi tendon organ mediates a disynaptic reflex arc via Type Ib afferent fibers; increased muscle tension activates the reflex, causing sudden muscle relaxation (autogenic inhibition) that protects against muscle tear at very high tensions, while also equalizing force among muscle fibers across the physiological range. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the neural circuit basis of the flexor withdrawal reflex?","choices":["Nociceptor activation transmitted to the cord synapses with interneurons that diverge to withdrawal muscles, inhibit antagonists, and activate reverberating circuits to prolong contraction","It only involves gamma motor neurons, adjusting muscle spindle sensitivity in the withdrawing limb rather than producing actual limb movement","It is a purely monosynaptic reflex with no interneurons at all, in which the nociceptor afferent synapses directly onto the alpha-motoneuron","It requires cortical input for every single occurrence, since the isolated spinal cord alone cannot generate a coordinated withdrawal response"],"answer":0,"exp":"The flexor withdrawal reflex begins with nociceptor activation transmitted to the spinal cord, which synapses with interneurons that diverge to stimulate withdrawal muscles, inhibit antagonist muscles, and activate reverberating circuits that prolong the muscle contraction (after-discharge). — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the function of the crossed-extensor reflex, and when does it begin relative to the flexor withdrawal reflex?","choices":["It has no consistent timing relationship to the flexor withdrawal reflex, occurring at random intervals from seconds to minutes after the painful stimulus","It occurs simultaneously with the flexor reflex in the very same limb, reinforcing rather than opposing the ongoing withdrawal movement","It elicits an extensor reflex in the opposite limb beginning 0.2-0.5 seconds after the painful stimulus (after the flexor reflex), pushing the body away and shifting weight to the opposite limb","It only occurs within the same limb as the painful stimulus, extending that limb further rather than shifting weight to the opposite side"],"answer":2,"exp":"The crossed-extensor reflex elicits an extensor reflex in the limb opposite to a painful stimulus, beginning 0.2-0.5 seconds after the stimulus (after the flexor withdrawal reflex) — this serves to push the body away from the stimulus and shift weight to the opposite limb. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How does the abundance of interneurons compare to anterior motor neurons, and what role do they play?","choices":["Interneurons and motor neurons exist in roughly equal numbers, with each anterior motor neuron paired one-to-one with its own dedicated interneuron","Interneurons are far less abundant than motor neurons, are large and poorly excitable, and play essentially no role in mediating spinal reflexes","Interneurons are about 30x more abundant than anterior motor neurons, are small and highly excitable, and comprise most of the neural circuitry for motor reflexes","Most signals descending from the brain bypass interneurons entirely, synapsing directly and exclusively onto anterior motor neurons themselves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Interneurons are about 30 times more abundant than anterior motor neurons, are small and highly excitable, and most signals from the brain terminate on interneurons, which comprise the neural circuitry for motor reflexes. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the function of propriospinal fibers?","choices":["They travel up and down the cord for 1-2 segments, providing pathways for multisegmental reflexes and contributing to proprioception","They exist only within the cervical enlargement of the spinal cord, coordinating reflexes exclusively between the arms and neck","They carry signals only from the brain down to the cord, having no intraspinal function or role in multisegmental reflex pathways","They exit the spinal cord entirely to innervate the skin, functioning as purely cutaneous sensory fibers rather than intraspinal ones"],"answer":0,"exp":"Propriospinal fibers travel up and down the spinal cord for 1-2 segments, providing pathways for multisegmental reflexes and playing an important role in proprioception. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why do muscles like the extraocular muscles and hand/neck muscles have a higher density of muscle spindles than muscles like the quadriceps?","choices":["Spindle density is entirely unrelated to the precision of movement a muscle performs, depending instead purely on its total cross-sectional area and bulk","Larger muscles always have proportionally more spindles than smaller ones, regardless of whether the movements they perform are coarse or fine","Larger muscles performing coarse movements need fewer spindles, while smaller muscles performing fine movements need many spindles for precise control","All muscles have identical spindle density regardless of size, since spindle number is fixed genetically rather than functionally determined"],"answer":2,"exp":"Muscle spindle density varies according to function: larger muscles with coarse movements (e.g., quadriceps) have relatively few spindles, while smaller muscles performing fine movements (e.g., extraocular muscles, hand and neck muscles) have many spindles for precise control. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What happens to muscle contraction smoothness when the sensory afferents from a muscle spindle are severed?","choices":["Muscle contraction becomes noticeably stronger and faster once severed, since the spindle normally exerts a braking effect that is removed along with it","Contraction remains perfectly smooth, since the smoothing function is actually provided by the Golgi tendon organ rather than the muscle spindle","The muscle can no longer contract at all, since alpha motor neuron output is thought to depend entirely on an intact afferent signal from the spindle","Muscle contraction becomes jerky, since the stretch reflex's smoothing function (via the muscle spindle) is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stretch reflex normally smooths out muscle contractions; when sensory afferents from a muscle spindle are sectioned, muscle contraction becomes noticeably jerky, demonstrating the muscle spindle system's smoothing function. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"In amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), which motor neurons deteriorate, and which are relatively spared?","choices":["Only gamma motor neurons deteriorate in ALS, sparing both the upper and lower alpha motor neurons throughout the entire course of the disease","Upper and lower alpha motor neurons deteriorate; gamma motor neurons are relatively spared","All motor neuron types, including alpha, gamma, and spinal interneurons, are affected equally and simultaneously right from disease onset","Interneurons deteriorate exclusively in ALS, while both alpha and gamma motor neurons remain completely functional throughout the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"In ALS (Lou Gehrig's disease), upper and lower alpha motor neurons deteriorate while gamma motor neurons are relatively spared — the gamma neurons may even contribute to disease progression by increasing muscle stiffness and spasticity via muscle spindle activation. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Does the Golgi tendon organ only signal muscle force at very high tension levels?","choices":["It has no functional signaling range at all, remaining completely silent until the muscle-tendon unit is on the verge of mechanical failure","It only responds to changes in muscle length, not tension, functioning as a redundant backup sensor duplicating the muscle spindle's role","Yes -- it only activates near the point of muscle tear, remaining completely silent throughout the normal physiological range of tension","No — it signals muscle force through the entire physiological range of tension, not just at high levels"],"answer":3,"exp":"Golgi tendon organs signal muscle force through the entire physiological range of tension (functioning to equalize force among muscle fibers), not only at the very high tension levels associated with the protective autogenic inhibition reflex. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Does activation of a Golgi tendon organ in one muscle affect adjacent muscles?","choices":["Yes — Golgi tendon organ activation is relayed through spinal interneurons to inhibit alpha motor neurons in every synergist of that limb","No — the Golgi tendon organ reflex only affects the individual muscle it is located in; adjacent muscles are not affected","Only in part — it inhibits its own agonist while simultaneously facilitating the antagonist muscle across the same joint","Yes, but only muscles sharing the same spinal segment are affected, via a fast monosynaptic reflex arc"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Golgi tendon organ reflex only affects an individual muscle — adjacent muscles are not affected by activation of a given Golgi tendon organ. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"At approximately what velocity is muscle length/tension information transmitted from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs to higher brain centers?","choices":["120 m/sec","1000 m/sec","0.5 m/sec","6 m/sec"],"answer":0,"exp":"Signals from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs are transmitted to higher centers at about 120 m/sec, informing the brain of instantaneous changes in muscle tension and length for feedback control of motor activity. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Where do the basic circuits for walking reflexes reside?","choices":["Only in the cerebral cortex","Only in the basal ganglia","Only in the cerebellum","In the spinal cord"],"answer":3,"exp":"Basic walking reflexes reside in the spinal cord itself, illustrating that the cord contains neuronal circuits for locomotion beyond simply conducting signals to and from higher centers. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is Westphal's sign, and what can cause it?","choices":["A pathologically exaggerated (hyperactive) patellar reflex caused specifically by cerebellar disease, without any accompanying muscle weakness","Absence or decrease of the patellar reflex, which can be caused by receptor damage, peripheral nerve disease, or lesions in the motor cortex/pyramidal tracts","A pathologically brisk, clonus-producing patellar reflex resulting from loss of descending inhibitory input from the cortex onto the spinal reflex arc","Hyperactivity of gamma motor neurons causing increased muscle spindle sensitivity and an exaggerated, brisk patellar reflex response"],"answer":1,"exp":"Westphal's sign is the absence or decrease of the patellar reflex, which can result from many causes including receptor damage, peripheral nerve disease, or a lesion in the motor cortex or pyramidal tracts. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What sign, seen during a reflex test like the patellar reflex, suggests cerebellar disease?","choices":["Multiple oscillations of the leg","A delayed reflex onset only","An unusually strong single kick with no oscillation","Complete absence of the reflex"],"answer":0,"exp":"Multiple oscillations of the leg during a reflex test (like the patellar reflex) is a sign of cerebellar disease, reflecting impaired damping of the motor response. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What would happen to the stretch reflex response if only the extrafusal muscle contracted without the intrafusal (spindle) fibers contracting?","choices":["The stretch response would become exaggerated due to unopposed gamma motor neuron drive","The spindle would fire continuously at its maximum discharge rate throughout the contraction","The spindle would go slack (unloaded), making the stretch response unavailable","The extrafusal fibers would fatigue rapidly and the reflex arc would be permanently disabled"],"answer":2,"exp":"If the extrafusal muscle contracts without corresponding contraction of the intrafusal fiber ends (i.e., without alpha-gamma co-activation), the spindle goes slack and the stretch response becomes temporarily unavailable — this is why co-activation is necessary during voluntary movement. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is proprioception?","choices":["The detection of chemical changes in the blood that regulate breathing rate","The sensing of the relative position of body parts and the strength of effort used in movement","The perception of two-point discrimination and fine touch localization on the skin","The sense of body orientation relative to gravity, mediated by the vestibular apparatus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Proprioception (from Latin proprius, \"one's own,\" and perception) is the sensing of the relative position of body parts and the strength of effort used in movement, contributed to significantly by propriospinal fibers, muscle spindles, and Golgi tendon organs. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Are signals from muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs mainly processed consciously or subconsciously?","choices":["Mainly consciously, requiring deliberate attention, since they bypass the cerebellum entirely and project only to the cortex","Equally consciously and subconsciously, with no particular bias toward either the cerebellum or intrinsic motor control","Only muscle spindle signals are processed subconsciously, while Golgi tendon organ signals require conscious attention","Mainly subconsciously, for intrinsic muscle control, though they also transmit information to the cerebrum and cerebellum"],"answer":3,"exp":"Signals from muscle spindle and Golgi tendon organ sensory receptors are used mainly for intrinsic (subconscious) muscle control, occurring mainly subconsciously, though they also transmit information to the cerebrum and cerebellum. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Where are motor neurons located in the spinal cord, and how do they compare in size to other neurons?","choices":["They are scattered diffusely throughout the gray matter and are the same size as interneurons","They are located in the anterior portion of the cord and are 50-100% larger than other neurons","They are located in the lateral horn only and are about 25% smaller than sensory neurons","They are located in the posterior (dorsal) horn and are 50% smaller than sensory neurons"],"answer":1,"exp":"Motor neurons are located in the anterior portion of the spinal cord and are 50-100% larger than other neurons, giving rise to nerve fibers that leave the cord via the anterior roots. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Once sensory fibers enter the spinal cord, what two general things can happen to their signal?","choices":["They are transmitted to higher centers, or they synapse locally to elicit motor reflexes","They travel exclusively to the thalamus and never synapse within the spinal cord itself","They are immediately degraded by local peptidases before reaching any synapse","They always ascend directly to the cerebral cortex without any local processing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sensory fibers entering the spinal cord are either transmitted onward to higher centers, or synapse locally within the cord to elicit motor reflexes — illustrating the cord's dual role in relay and local reflex processing. — Lecture 4 - Nervous System - Motor-2.pptx, Slide 4"}]};
